# Balanced new evidence: support, contradiction, mixed evidence and the cost of delay.
STRESS = {
10:'A peak-load failover rehearsal now passes using the current release and the registration request mix. An independent reconciliation of the inspected registrations finds no lost or duplicate confirmations. The registrar authorizes a documented alternative submission route if errors recur. These are supplied teaching-case observations, not a guarantee about future operation. Reassess whether continuing under your original boundary is justified; explain any remaining uncertainty.',
11:'The detailed operational profile now shows that retry-heavy requests were under-represented in the reported test. The registrar also confirms that a full stop would prevent students from completing time-limited payments, while a staffed alternative has limited capacity. Keep the observed 6/20,000 calculation, but reassess its scope and compare the consequences of release, restriction and delay. No further test result is supplied.',
12:'A daylight replay shows that a missing camera frame is treated as an empty zone, and the stop button shares the controller power supply. No accident is reported. Identify the shared cause and reassess the hazard argument without inventing a probability or an executed repair.',
13:'A reviewer supplies synthetic-account tests of both preview and bulk-export endpoints. Cross-user requests are rejected server-side on both paths; allowed requests succeed. The report identifies the tested release and roles, but does not include a review of sensitive information in logs. Explain which concern is resolved, which remains, and whether your original action should be retained, relaxed or narrowed.',
14:'An outage rehearsal disconnects the central sign-in service. Authorized staff successfully access the protected local pickup list using the documented offline procedure. Paper changes are reconciled after recovery with no discrepancy in the rehearsal sample. The evidence covers the tested desk, trained staff and daytime workload. Explain whether this supports your original fallback boundary and what it does not establish.',
15:'The vendor confirms that Option A’s export excludes recurring-booking rules and approval history. Option B may be extended, but its owner still supplies no peak-load measurements. The pilot owner also states that delaying all evaluation until every uncertainty is removed would miss the next-term pilot; a narrowly scoped evaluation of one option is possible. Compare the cost of delay with the unresolved lifecycle risks.',
16:'A trace outside the small duration-test model shows that an already-reserved room returns its existing reservation identifier without signaling a conflict. No second reservation was created. The brief did not document this behavior. Explain why your executed duration tests do or do not address this observation, and revise the success postcondition or request a documented contract.',
17:'A separate reviewed failover trace now shows that request identifiers and their outcomes are stored atomically with reservations in persistent storage. Replaying the same identifier after restart returns the existing outcome without a second reservation. This evidence covers a single booking store; it does not cover external payment side effects. Compare it with your simulator results and state whether retaining a bounded retry policy is justified.',
20:'Transport changes location updates from every minute to every 20 minutes without changing the API shape. Security observations remain current. The pilot owner cannot force transport to revert, and facilities still benefits from current security incidents even without transport locations. Decide whether to retain, narrow or replace the shared claim, and identify what remains useful.'
}

def pages(n):
 return [p for _,a,b in STUDY[n]['readings'] for p in range(a,b+1)]
